<p>If you tried to source de-oiled rice bran (DORB) from India between mid-2023 and October 2025 and came away with nothing, the reason is simple: export was prohibited. The same prohibition explains why published trade statistics look implausibly small for a product India makes at real scale. DGFT Notification No. 37/2025-26, effective 3 October 2025, lifted the prohibition and returned DORB to Free export status. This update is for feed mills, feed-ingredient traders and livestock integrators who used Indian DORB before the ban or are considering it for the first time. It covers what changed, how to read the numbers, which grade parameters to specify, and what to expect when you rebuild the supply chain.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<p><strong>Don't read the market-data table above as normal demand.</strong> The figures are real, primary-tier WITS/UN Comtrade data, but they are easy to misread. Calendar-2024 DORB exports total just USD 417,150 across five destinations: Nepal, Sweden, Bangladesh, Germany and the Maldives. That is almost negligible for a product produced at DORB's normal scale. The ban was in force for the whole calendar year, so the figure reflects the ban, not weak demand, limited Indian supply or a small buyer base. Nepal, Bangladesh and other regional land and short-sea markets are the natural high-volume destinations once trade normalises, based on general feed-ingredient trade patterns in the region. That is a reasonable expectation, not something the 2024 numbers show. Full post-ban 2026 data has not been published yet. Until the table is refreshed with post-liberalisation figures, treat it as a record of the ban period, not a guide to market size.</p>

<h2>Why it was banned</h2>
<p>DORB is an important cattle-feed ingredient in India's own dairy and livestock sector. The export restriction, in place from July 2023, was brought in to protect domestic supply and keep feed costs manageable for Indian farmers. This is a recurring pattern in Indian agricultural export policy. When a commodity matters for domestic food or feed security, the government uses export restrictions fairly readily, sometimes with little advance notice.</p>

<h2>The distinction that matters most: DORB vs. raw rice bran</h2>
<p>This is the easiest mistake to make when coming back to this market. DORB (de-oiled rice bran, HS 2302.40 / 2306.90) is what remains after oil is solvent-extracted from raw rice bran: a defatted residue high in fibre and protein. Raw or boiled rice bran (HS 2302.20) is a different product under a different customs heading. It remains Restricted and can be exported only under licence. The ban-lift applies to DORB only. If a supplier offers "rice bran" without naming the product and HS code, confirm before you order, because the wrong product may not be able to clear customs legally.</p>

<h2>DORB grade parameters to put in your purchase order</h2>
<p>For a feed mill this is a genuine reopening of a supply option, not a marginal development. We can quote DORB for export now, under the same specification and documentation process as any other feed ingredient. A feed-grade DORB contract is specified on five parameters:</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table class="data-table">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Parameter</th><th scope="col">Typical range</th><th scope="col">Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Crude protein</td><td>14-16%</td><td>The main nutritional value in the feed formulation, and the first figure to compare between offers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crude fibre</td><td>≤12-14%</td><td>Limits how much the mill can include in a ration, especially for poultry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Moisture</td><td>≤12%</td><td>Higher moisture raises the risk of mould and caking in storage and transit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Residual crude fat/oil</td><td>≤1.5-2%</td><td>Shows how complete the extraction was, and drives rancidity risk and shelf life</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sand/silica</td><td>≤15% max, grade-dependent</td><td>Varies most between processing batches, is the most common source of grade disputes, and is the main reason one grade prices differently from another</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Packing is in 25kg or 50kg PP bags, 1 MT jumbo bags (FIBC), or bulk vessel loading for large contracts. DORB has a low bulk density, so a bagged 20ft container holds roughly 18-20 MT and a 40ft roughly 22-24 MT. Tell us your target protein and fibre, and any sand/silica ceiling, and we will quote against that grade rather than a generic one.</p>

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>Because DORB's export status has changed twice in just over two years, the export paperwork should show the current policy position. The invoice and shipping documents need to state HS 2302.40 or 2306.90 and describe the product as de-oiled rice bran, not "rice bran" alone. On the destination side, feed-ingredient import registration is the importer's responsibility and varies by country. Confirm it before booking, especially if your registration dates from before the ban. Indian export policy on feed and food commodities has changed with little notice before, as this ban and its lifting both show. Confirm current DORB status when you place each order rather than assuming Free status is permanent, the same caution we apply to onion, wheat and other policy-sensitive products. Share your compliance checklist and we will confirm the documentation for your shipment.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>What will likely determine the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>Because DORB export has only just reopened, near-term pricing behaviour is less established than for a continuously-traded commodity — treat any benchmark from before July 2023 as a starting reference point, not a current quote. What's likely to matter once trade normalises: domestic Indian cattle-feed demand competing for the same raw material (DORB doesn't stop being useful inside India just because export reopened, so domestic and export demand will compete for supply), protein and fibre grade, sand/silica content (higher-tolerance material typically prices lower), currency, and freight to your specific destination. We'd rather tell you honestly that the post-ban price picture is still forming than quote a number that implies more certainty than currently exists.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your specific grade, packaging and destination port. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us: your target protein/fibre spec, any sand/silica ceiling, packaging preference, quantity, and destination port.</p>
