<h2>What to expect re-establishing a DORB supply chain after a two-year gap</h2>
<p>If you sourced DORB from India before July 2023 and are coming back to it now, treat this as a fresh setup rather than picking up where you left off. Supplier relationships, documentation templates and specification agreements from more than two years ago shouldn't be assumed to still apply — verify current specification, current documentation requirements, and current supplier capability rather than working from memory of the pre-ban market.</p>
<p>The DORB-vs-raw-rice-bran distinction covered above isn't just a labelling nuance — it's a real, common ordering mistake. A buyer who specifies "rice bran" generically, or works from an old purchase order template that predates the ban-lift notification, risks a supplier quoting or shipping the wrong HS-code product. Always confirm HS code 2302.40 or 2306.90 specifically, not just the product name.</p>
<p>On quality: DORB carries residual oil even after solvent extraction, which makes it more prone to rancidity than whole grains — plan shipments around reasonably prompt turnover (3-6 months) rather than long-term storage, and specify sand/silica tolerance clearly, since this varies more by processing batch than protein content does and is a common source of grade disputes.</p>
<p>What first-time and returning buyers most often get wrong: assuming the market has already "normalised" simply because the ban is lifted. Supply chains, freight routings and typical destination volumes are still re-establishing themselves post-ban — build extra lead time and verification into your first order rather than assuming it will move as smoothly as an established, continuously-traded commodity would.</p>
